 "note": {
  "status": "draft",
  "text": "A backup is a copy of data kept in another place so that it can be restored if the original is lost.\nCauses of data loss: hardware failure (a dead hard disk), theft or loss of a device, viruses and ransomware, accidental deletion, fire, flood, power cuts.\nWhere to back up: an external hard disk or USB flash drive, a memory card, another computer, or the cloud (Google Drive, OneDrive).\nTypes: full backup (everything), incremental backup (only what changed since the last backup).\nThe 3-2-1 rule: keep 3 copies of important data, on 2 different kinds of storage, with 1 copy in another place (for example in the cloud).\nBack up regularly and test that you can restore the files.",
  "checks": []
 },
 "worked_example": {
  "status": "draft",
  "vars": {},
  "problem": "A pupil keeps all her school work only on her phone. Give two risks and one solution.",
  "steps": [
   "Risks: the phone can be stolen, or break, and all the work is lost.",
   "Solution: back up the files to the cloud or to a computer regularly."
  ],
  "answer": "Theft or breakdown; back up to the cloud or a computer.",
  "checks": []
 },
